    </style>
</head>
<body>

<div class="header">
    <h1>Generative AI Competitive Landscape</h1>
    <h2>User Sentiment & Complaint Analysis Across Top 5 AI Apps (Q1 2026)</h2>
    <p>Prepared for: Product Strategy Team</p>
</div>

<div class="section" id="executive-overview">
    <h2>1. Executive Overview</h2>
    <p>This report analyzes 50,000 user reviews (10,000 per app) across the top five generative AI mobile applications: ChatGPT, Microsoft Copilot, Google Gemini, Perplexity, and Claude. The objective is to identify key user pain points, sentiment patterns, and opportunities for strategic differentiation.</p>

    <h3>Top 3 Strategic Takeaways</h3>
    <ol>
        <li><strong>App-Specific Pain Points Dominate Discontent:</strong> While 'General' positive feedback is common, the distribution of specific complaints (Bugs, Accuracy, Pricing) varies drastically between competitors. Addressing the leading complaint theme for our app presents the highest ROI for user retention.</li>
        <li><strong>Sentiment-Rating Divergences Expose Complex User Emotions:</strong> Across all apps, there is a notable percentage of reviews where star ratings contradict the text's sentiment (e.g., sarcastic 5-star reviews or frustrated users giving high ratings due to app potential). Reliance on star ratings alone masks actual user frustration.</li>
        <li><strong>Negative Experiences Drive Longer Reviews:</strong> Users write significantly more when expressing dissatisfaction (1-star reviews are longer on average). Furthermore, reviews regarding bugs or accuracy receive higher 'thumbs up' engagement, indicating shared frustration among the user base.</li>
    </ol>
</div>

<div class="section" id="app-sentiment-comparison">
    <h2>2. Cross-App Sentiment Comparison</h2>
    <p>We evaluated user satisfaction using two metrics: traditional average star ratings and text-derived mean VADER sentiment polarity.</p>

